1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:05,371
Imagine the whole history of Earth squeezed into a single day.

2
00:00:06,121 --> 00:00:09,235
At midnight, a young planet forms from dust and fire.

3
00:00:09,985 --> 00:00:13,889
We humans arrive in the last six seconds before the next midnight.

4
00:00:14,639 --> 00:00:18,330
In between, life built itself, one level on top of another.

5
00:00:19,080 --> 00:00:21,704
Let’s watch it happen, in a world we simulate.

6
00:00:24,754 --> 00:00:28,615
Within the first hours, there are oceans. Nothing lives in them yet.

7
00:00:29,365 --> 00:00:35,317
This is a real simulation, not a drawing. Every patch of water is calculated as it runs.

8
00:00:36,067 --> 00:00:40,099
The sun warms the shallows. Tides fill pools and leave them to dry.

9
00:00:40,849 --> 00:00:44,732
On the deep floor, hot vents leak heat and minerals from below.

10
00:00:45,482 --> 00:00:49,898
As a pool dries, small molecules crowd together and join into chains.

11
00:00:50,648 --> 00:00:57,475
A few chains help make copies of themselves. Nothing here is alive, but something has started to pass itself on.

12
00:01:02,025 --> 00:01:04,499
By four or five in the morning, there are cells.

13
00:01:05,249 --> 00:01:09,089
Each one eats, divides when it is full, and dies when it runs out.

14
00:01:09,839 --> 00:01:14,383
Daughters copy their genes with small mistakes. Nobody designs anything.

15
00:01:15,133 --> 00:01:20,573
Yet cells that only drifted begin to swim, because the ones that swim leave more daughters.

16
00:01:21,323 --> 00:01:26,507
In our runs, the motor gene grows about seven times, then settles at a best strength.

17
00:01:31,057 --> 00:01:34,833
Then come predators: big red cells that swallow anything small.

18
00:01:35,583 --> 00:01:39,445
A daughter that stays stuck to its mother makes a clump too big to swallow.

19
00:01:40,195 --> 00:01:44,504
So the gene for sticking spreads, even though clumped cells must share their food.

20
00:01:45,254 --> 00:01:49,393
Real algae do the same in experiments, in a few hundred generations.

21
00:01:53,943 --> 00:01:57,527
Then, for most of the day, microbes rule the planet alone.

22
00:01:58,277 --> 00:02:03,525
Before noon, some of them fill the air with oxygen, and the sky and seas change for ever.

23
00:02:04,275 --> 00:02:08,072
In the evening, ice spreads almost to the equator, then melts again.

24
00:02:08,822 --> 00:02:11,745
Only now do cells team up into large bodies.

25
00:02:14,795 --> 00:02:16,907
Cells that stay together take on roles.

26
00:02:17,657 --> 00:02:21,966
Some squeeze, like the first muscle cells. Others only make new cells.

27
00:02:22,716 --> 00:02:28,753
A body moves only when its squeezing cells work in step. Out of step, it just sloshes in place.

28
00:02:29,503 --> 00:02:36,138
Then some cells start passing signals. A net of nerves, with no centre, tells the whole body where the food is.

29
00:02:40,688 --> 00:02:43,781
Around nine in the evening, the sea fills with animals.

30
00:02:44,531 --> 00:02:50,867
Our creatures descend from those bodies. Each has a small brain of neurons that learns what is good to eat.

31
00:02:51,617 --> 00:02:57,249
Then hunters appear. Prey that notice them in time survive, and hunters that chase well eat.

32
00:02:57,999 --> 00:03:04,527
Too many hunters, and they empty the sea and starve. Balance is not given. It emerges, or it fails.

33
00:03:09,077 --> 00:03:12,149
Everything you just saw is one world. We call it Gaia.

34
00:03:12,899 --> 00:03:18,169
The same coast and the same chemistry, seen at different levels, from molecules to brains.

35
00:03:18,919 --> 00:03:23,057
Each level is built from the one below, and none of it is drawn by hand.

36
00:03:23,807 --> 00:03:30,314
Now pick a moment and go deeper. Each one has its own film, and a lab where you change the rules yourself.

